  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A front will drop through the forecast area \ntoday, bringing rain showers and isolated thunderstorms to the \nregion this afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front will the forecast area this afternoon, bringing \nanother round of showers and storms across the entire area. CAMs\nforecast reflectivity suggest that there could be a prefrontal\ntrough ahead of the main front, such that showers and storms\ndevelop across the Crown of Maine along with the Interior\nDowneast region at the same time early this afternoon before the\nfront proceeds southwards through the CWA. Though forecast 0-6 \nkm shear is quite high, approaching 50 kts from the NAMNEST, \nforecast CAPE is on the lower end, around 500 J/kg from the \nNAMNEST, and there will not be any deep layer of moisture to \nwork with. That said, thunderstorms are possible but will likely\nbe ordinary in structure. Lines of training storms could \ndevelop along the front and prefrontal boundaries themselves as\nthey cross the area, and this would be the best chance at any \nstronger organization for enhanced threats, but even then, at \nmost it appears maybe some gusty winds with these storms based \non the lack of extensive severe parameters being met at this \ntime.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...System brings round of rain showers mid-to-late\nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure over northern New England early week, as a low \npressure moves eastward through Canada. Chance for some stray \nshowers moving into the Central Highlands and Bangor regions on\nTuesday, as some models hint at a disturbance developing off of\nthe aforementioned parent low in Canada. \n\nFor rain mid-to-late week, global models are trending towards \nthe low pressure system moving through Maine Wednesday night \ninto Thursday. The disagreement between models is currently in \na surface low that develops off of the parent low in Canada. The\nGFS has a surface low develop on Thursday, and staying out in \nthe Atlantic. The ECMWF, however, has a surface low develop \nmuch earlier on Tuesday, over the Gulf of Maine, and slowly \nmove out to sea through the week. With either solution, \nanticipating highest rain totals along the Downeast region. \nConfidence in timing, severe potential, and rain amounts, will \nbegin to improve once models get a better handle on the \nenvironment for next week.",
